WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.102
You now know how ColPali works,

00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:04.671
how its memory footprint can be optimized,

00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:07.774
and how MUVERA speeds up multi-vector search.

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:10.677
Let's put all these different pieces together

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:13.780
and build a functioning multi-modal RAG system

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:17.250
based on ColPali. Let's build something.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.053
In previous lessons, we've seen how ColPali enables

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:23.456
visual document retrieval through multi-vector embeddings.

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:27.060
Now, you will build a complete RAG system

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.062
that combines ColPali's retrieval

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:31.731
with OpenAI's vision models for generation.

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:35.735
This approach lets you answer questions about documents

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:38.438
by retrieving relevant pages

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:42.242
and having a vision capable language model read them directly.

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:44.444
No OCR needed.

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:46.279
You will start by loading the

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:48.381
OpenAI API key from the environmental file

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:51.284
and creating an OpenAI client.

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:56.056
You will recreate the collection from lesson three that contains ColPali embeddings

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:58.158
with various optimizations.

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:01.261
This ensures you can run this lesson independently

00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:03.363
without completing lesson three first.

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:06.966
The collection includes multiple optimization strategies.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:10.470
original embeddings, binary and scalar quantization,

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.273
and different kinds of pooling.

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.775
In the previous lesson, you explored MUVERA,

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:19.579
which compresses ColPali's variable length multi-vector embeddings

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:21.781
into fixed dimensional encodings.

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:25.885
This enables HNSW indexing for faster search at scale.

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:29.222
You will add MUVERA embeddings to the same collection

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:31.524
to demonstrate two-stage retrieval,

00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:34.494
using MUVERA for fast candidate retrieval,

00:01:34.794 --> 00:01:38.231
then reranking with ColPali for maximum accuracy.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.867
Let's create a MUVERA instance with the

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:43.103
same configuration like in the previous lesson.

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:46.506
As a next step, you will recreate the Qdrant collection

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:50.810
with all the memory optimizations, including MUVERA embeddings.

00:01:51.211 --> 00:01:54.214
You have another helper function to achieve exactly this.

00:01:54.681 --> 00:01:58.885
The recreate_colpali_optimizations_collection helper function

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.489
imported all the memory optimizations we discussed

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:04.691
in one of the previous lessons,

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:07.494
and also created MUVERA representations

00:02:07.494 --> 00:02:09.896
for the original ColPali multi-vectors.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:12.098
It's time for queries.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:16.069
This helper function will retrieve precomputed vector embeddings

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:18.671
for a list of  interesting queries

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:21.674
that you can use to test your RAG system.

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:24.377
You can now test the helper function with a sample question

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.679
about the documents in the collection.

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:28.848
Instead of embedding queries separately

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:31.651
and passing them to search functions,

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:33.753
you will create a unified retriever

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:36.556
that handles query embedding internally

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:40.560
and returns a list of image path and score tuples.

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:44.464
The retrieve function takes a textual query

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:48.067
and sets using parameter

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:51.771
of the query_points method of Qdrant's SDK,

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:54.707
to a specific vector name that we want

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:57.010
to use for retrieval in that particular case.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:02.215
Because some of the optimization methods we discussed earlier are quantization methods,

00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:05.418
and Qdrant has a rescoring function built in.

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:09.656
We specifically say that we don't want to use the rescoring mechanism

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:12.158
because otherwise we could get better results

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:14.994
than just with quantized vectors.

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:18.598
Another helper function will run search

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:20.700
with two-stage retrieval.

00:03:20.967 --> 00:03:23.269
It will use MUVERA embeddings

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.272
as an initial prefetch mechanism,

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:29.776
and then it will rerank with the vectors

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:31.978
that you want to use at that stage.

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:34.681
Obviously, we could rescore

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:36.683
with the original ColPali embeddings.

00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:39.385
However, if we are able to use

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:40.987
float vectors,

00:03:40.987 --> 00:03:42.989
we could also save the memory

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:44.991
and still get decent results

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:46.893
out of this two-stage retrieval.

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:49.495
So we are actually going to test both

00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:51.464
MUVERA for faster retrieval.

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:54.834
and different kinds of memory optimizations

00:03:54.834 --> 00:03:57.437
to get the best out of both worlds.

00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:00.740
Let's test our retriever function with a sample query.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.144
The retrieval will handle the query embedding generation internally,

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:07.747
and will return the top results.

00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:11.484
Here are the top PDF pages for

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:13.853
the query, How does the human brain work?

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:19.692
Now let's focus on the generation part.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.962
You will send the retrieved document pages to GPT-4o

00:04:23.263 --> 00:04:25.164
along with the user's question.

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:28.701
GPT-4o can read text, diagrams, and formulas

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:31.404
directly from the images to generate accurate answers.

00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:34.107
There are different parts of that process.

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:36.509
First of all, we are sending a system prompt.

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:39.912
telling the model to answer our questions

00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:42.915
based only on the provided document images.

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:45.718
We also want to have an answer generated

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:48.321
as a Markdown so we can just render that properly.

00:04:48.588 --> 00:04:52.892
Then, we'll pass the original prompt sent to the system.

00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:55.895
Finally, you will attach all the images

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:58.097
provided to this particular function.

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:02.435
At the very end, this entire interaction will be sent to the

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:04.737
OpenAI service

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:07.740
to get the LLM output.

00:05:08.441 --> 00:05:11.511
Let's test the generation with the previously retrieved documents

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:15.114
and see how GPT-4o answers our question.

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:19.419
Nice. You've just implemented a retrieval augmented generation

00:05:19.419 --> 00:05:22.822
that works directly on visual documents.

00:05:23.356 --> 00:05:27.460
Let's test our complete RAG workflow with several different questions.

00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:31.564
First of all, you will retrieve documents for all the queries

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:34.167
using the original ColPali retriever,

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:37.270
extract the image paths and generate the answer

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.972
using GPT-4o's vision capabilities.

00:05:40.373 --> 00:05:45.278
This two-step approach, retrieve and generate, using separate functions makes the process

00:05:45.278 --> 00:05:48.648
explicit and give us full control over each stage.

00:05:49.449 --> 00:05:54.053
You are not only displaying the answer as generated by the LLM,

00:05:54.454 --> 00:05:57.757
but also the input images that we sent

00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:00.860
along with the prompt.

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:03.763
The same goes for the second query.

00:06:03.996 --> 00:06:06.065
from our dataset.

00:06:18.478 --> 00:06:20.480
And the last query.

00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:28.621
At this point, it shouldn't be surprising

00:06:28.621 --> 00:06:31.023
that original ColPali vectors did a pretty good job.

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:34.060
However, in practice, you will typically

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.062
try to optimize that process

00:06:36.162 --> 00:06:38.030
using all the techniques that we learned.

00:06:38.231 --> 00:06:42.435
And for that, you store multiple optimized vectors in the same collection

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:45.438
so you can just test different optimization methods.

00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:47.974
Let's test some of the techniques we

00:06:47.974 --> 00:06:50.042
used in one of the previous lessons.

00:06:50.443 --> 00:06:53.246
You will specifically use binary quantized vectors

00:06:53.446 --> 00:06:57.016
and hierarchical token pooling with pool factors set to two.

00:06:57.417 --> 00:07:00.219
If you now test the same retriever

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.923
but using different optimization techniques,

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.893
you should see some different outputs

00:07:06.993 --> 00:07:10.997
because the input documents will be obviously slightly different.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.033
Here's the answer generated for the Binary Quantized

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:16.536
version of our embeddings.

00:07:19.839 --> 00:07:23.142
Even though it didn't use the same set of images.

00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:27.580
It still provided the retrieval response to the input prompt.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.385
Hierarchical token pooling was doing even a better job in our experiments.

00:07:33.085 --> 00:07:35.388
And not surprisingly, it also did a good job.

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:39.592
However, memory optimization is typically not enough.

00:07:40.059 --> 00:07:41.928
As we saw in the previous lesson,

00:07:42.728 --> 00:07:44.931
MUVERA alone can have lower precision

00:07:44.931 --> 00:07:47.733
compared to the original ColPali multi-vectors,

00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:50.236
but it offers significantly faster search

00:07:50.236 --> 00:07:52.438
through HNSW indexing.

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:55.241
The optimal production approach combines both.

00:07:55.741 --> 00:07:58.744
Use MUVERA to quickly retrieve a broader set of candidates,

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:00.746
and then re rank those candidates

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:04.150
with any optimization method for the final results.

00:08:04.484 --> 00:08:08.387
That's why you created a retrieve with two-stage function

00:08:08.688 --> 00:08:12.992
that implements this pattern using Qdrant's prefetch mechanism

00:08:13.493 --> 00:08:16.596
with a flexible reranking strategy to control it.

00:08:16.929 --> 00:08:18.731
Let's see it in action.

00:08:19.232 --> 00:08:21.734
Let's test the two-stage retrieval

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:24.837
and compare it with the original ColPali retriever.

00:08:25.137 --> 00:08:29.041
For this comparison, you will use the default reranking strategy

00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:32.144
with original full ColPali embeddings.

00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:34.647
after MUVERA's fast prefetch.

00:08:34.647 --> 00:08:37.149
That should hopefully maintain high accuracy while

00:08:37.149 --> 00:08:40.653
benefiting from MUVERA's HNSW search,

00:08:40.653 --> 00:08:44.657
which is especially important when scaling to larger document collections.

00:08:45.258 --> 00:08:49.662
Let's see how the MUVERA two-stage retrieval results look like.

00:08:51.998 --> 00:08:54.133
The original ColPali retriever does a really

00:08:54.133 --> 00:08:56.302
good job, but this is our baseline.

00:08:57.203 --> 00:09:01.707
And two-stage retrieval with MUVERA and ColPali reranking is actually using

00:09:01.908 --> 00:09:04.210
a very good set of input images,

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:07.914
so the output of the LLM is grounded

00:09:07.914 --> 00:09:09.916
in the knowledge we have.

00:09:10.716 --> 00:09:14.420
And while MUVERA handles fast prefetch with HNSW indexing,

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:17.423
the reranking stage can use any optimization method,

00:09:17.423 --> 00:09:19.825
not necessarily the original vectors.

00:09:20.226 --> 00:09:25.031
As a last step, let's compare how this different reranking strategies

00:09:25.031 --> 00:09:27.233
affect retrieval quality and performance.

00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:31.437
When we run this experiment, the documents retrieved by MUVERA

00:09:31.437 --> 00:09:36.342
will be reranked using Original ColPali vectors, Binary Quantized ones,

00:09:36.509 --> 00:09:38.878
and hierarchical token pooling.

00:09:39.645 --> 00:09:42.348
Each reranking strategy offers different trade-offs.

00:09:43.149 --> 00:09:45.618
Original ColPali provides the highest accuracy

00:09:45.618 --> 00:09:48.487
with full max computation across all multi-vectors.

00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:52.491
However, binary quantization offers faster reranking

00:09:53.025 --> 00:09:55.227
by using compressed representation

00:09:55.227 --> 00:09:57.229
and it also significantly reduces

00:09:57.229 --> 00:10:00.800
the amount of memory needed to keep all the vectors.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.902
Though, with slightly reduced precision.

00:10:03.336 --> 00:10:05.438
hierarchical token pooling

00:10:05.838 --> 00:10:08.541
reduces the number of vectors to compare with

00:10:08.841 --> 00:10:13.512
during reranking, providing another speed accuracy balance point.

00:10:14.213 --> 00:10:16.916
In production, you may choose binary quantization

00:10:17.116 --> 00:10:19.118
for cost-sensitive applications

00:10:19.318 --> 00:10:22.822
or stick with original ColPali when maximum accuracy is critical.

00:10:23.255 --> 00:10:26.158
In this lesson, we built a complete multimodal RAG system

00:10:26.258 --> 00:10:28.861
combining ColPali's visual retrieval

00:10:28.961 --> 00:10:31.063
with OpenAI's vision models.

00:10:31.263 --> 00:10:33.366
The two-stage retrieval pattern using MUVERA

00:10:33.466 --> 00:10:36.135
is particularly powerful for production systems.

00:10:36.235 --> 00:10:38.838
It combines the speed of HNSW indexes

00:10:39.238 --> 00:10:41.440
while reranking is quite flexible

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.843
as we can use any optimization method.

00:10:44.143 --> 00:10:47.313
Throughout the entire course, you learned how to build a retrieval system

00:10:47.313 --> 00:10:49.415
working directly on scanned documents,

00:10:49.415 --> 00:10:51.317
PDFs, or images.

00:10:52.018 --> 00:10:54.620
There are two key issues related to multi-vectors.

00:10:55.021 --> 00:10:57.823
The first is high memory usage

00:10:57.823 --> 00:10:59.692
thanks to all those vectors. You

00:10:59.692 --> 00:11:01.894
can mitigate this issue by using quantization

00:11:01.894 --> 00:11:05.798
or pooling techniques or even by combining multiple methods together.

00:11:06.165 --> 00:11:09.268
The second problem is the fact that multi-vector representations

00:11:09.268 --> 00:11:14.173
are not compatible with the most popular approximate nearest neighbors techniques

00:11:14.173 --> 00:11:15.975
such as HNSW.

00:11:16.375 --> 00:11:18.944
So, by default, they require a full scan

00:11:18.944 --> 00:11:22.448
that doesn't scale. MUVERA is a promising method

00:11:22.448 --> 00:11:25.251
of converting variable length sequences of vectors

00:11:25.251 --> 00:11:27.653
into fixed dimensional representations,

00:11:27.653 --> 00:11:29.955
enabling approximate search.

00:11:30.456 --> 00:11:33.459
Typically, original vectors are still used for reranking

00:11:33.459 --> 00:11:35.961
to achieve both speed and high quality.

00:11:36.462 --> 00:11:38.264
Putting all these tools together,

00:11:38.464 --> 00:11:43.235
it's possible to build a flexible and high-performing multimodal RAG system,

00:11:43.235 --> 00:11:44.937
as you just saw.
